{{also|pää-}}
==Finnish==
{{Wiktionary:Picture dictionary/fi:head}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|fi|urj-fin-pro|*pää}}, from {{inh|fi|fiu-pro|*päŋe}}. Cognates include {{cog|et|pea}}, {{cog|vep|pä}}, [[Moksha]] and {{cog|myv|пе||end}} and {{cog|hu|fej}}, {{m|hu|fő}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{fi-pronunciation}}

===Noun===
{{fi-noun}}

# [[head]] {{gloss|part of a body}}
#: {{coi|fi|ahtaa '''päähänsä'''|to cram (information) '''into one's head''', to study hard}}
# [[head]] {{gloss|leader, expert, e.g. of an organisation}}
# [[head]] {{gloss|part in a machine}}
# [[end]], [[tip]] {{gloss|physical end, especially when there is no clear "beginning" and "end" but only two ends}}
# {{lb|fi|in external locative cases|in expressions}} [[mood]]
#: {{coi|fi|hyvällä '''päällä'''|in a good '''mood'''}}

====Usage notes====
* "'''on''' one's head" is translated with the internal locative cases (inessive, illative, elative) into Finnish when referring to headgear:
*: {{ux|fi|Miehellä oli hattu '''päässään'''.|The man had a hat '''on his head'''. / The man wore a hat.}}
* example of adessive case usage:
*: {{ux|fi|Mies seisoi '''päällään'''.|The man was standing '''on his head'''.}}
* adessive ({{m|fi|päällä}}), ablative ({{m|fi|päältä}}) and allative ({{m|fi|päälle}}) cases have also adverbial and postpositional usage; see respective entries
* inessive ({{m|fi|päässä}}), elative ({{m|fi|päästä}}) and illative ({{m|fi|päähän}}) cases have also postpositional usage relating to distance; see respective entries

